A||||27||V8Dreamer||R-134a conversion kits work? (jc whitney)||||1||
Z||000000||V8Dreamer||06-12-2001||04:03 PM||||I recently saw the $29.99 r-134a conversion kits in a jc whitney catalog and was wondering if anyone here has used them and what the success rate was like if so.  Please let me know since it is getting hot here, and it's getting to be less and less fun sweating in my seats while doing 70 down the highway.<P>Thanks,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||06-12-2001||04:38 PM||||I've used several of the Intermatic's kits (I believe that is what JC Whitney sells) with good results.  I've gotten them from WalMart, KMart, and AutoZone.  So far so good.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||06-12-2001||06:25 PM||||Same here!<BR>Save the shipping and the wait, run on over to Wally World!||||reg||1
Z||000003||DecadenceR||06-12-2001||06:56 PM||||Theres really a wally world???<BR>Every time I hear that I think of National Lampoons Vacation... LOL<BR>"We're going to Wally World!!!!"||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||06-12-2001||07:54 PM||||LOL!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||06-12-2001||07:59 PM||||I've done both my Fiero and my Volvo with the $30 kits.  My AC is cold as $hit now.  They work great.  just make sure you drain as much old oil out of the system as possible.  But don't pull your hair out trying.  It's not critical to get every last drop out.  The 2 oils DO NOT react negatively with each other.  Also, if you can, make sure you evacuate the system for at least an hour to evaporate any possible moisture.||||reg||14
Z||000006||maryjane||06-12-2001||08:55 PM||||Related to this topic..I've seen some freon for sale at Checkers auto called Freeze-12.<BR>It is about $6.50 a can and is supposedly a replacement for R-12. You don't need a license to buy it. Anybody know more about this stuff?||||reg||5
Z||000007||Jncomutt||06-12-2001||09:17 PM||||Freeze-12 info can be found at <A HREF="http://www.freeze12.com" TARGET=_blank>www.freeze12.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>17 year old Fierophile<BR>'86 Coupe 160k miles<BR>'84 Mint Beauty 51k and original tires!||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||06-12-2001||10:26 PM||||mmmmm. never heard of Freeze 12, gonna have to investigate. I like R12 better, I still say its colder. My Fiero, still with R12 will freeze you out on a 90* day, you have to turn it down. My other car 94 Lumina with 134* is barely cool coming out of the dash. When its 95* you have to run recirculate to keep cool at all. It was just serviced too, so its full and working properly. (wonders if I can put R12 in my Lumina?)||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||06-12-2001||10:39 PM||||I've done the 134 conversion on my Fiero and a Trans Sport minivan.  Both worked great!  Absolutely no complaints. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Songman||06-13-2001||03:30 AM||||I converted my 88 T-top today and will be doing my Indy tomorrow. I love these little kits. Advance Auto also has them on sale for $29.99. <P>It is true that 134 is not as cold as R12, but we are talking about Fieros here. It gets plenty cold to keep that little space kinda chilly even.<P>The Indy will make number 8 of my cars that I have converted in the last year... Kinda hard to beat it with the prices on R12 these days.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||14
Z||000011||deceler8||06-13-2001||09:03 AM||||The kits work, and there are some things you should do if you want them to keep working.<P>Among them...get as much old oil out as possible. Blow out the condenser with compressed air, the lines, use a new dryer, and check/clean or replace the orifice tube.<P>The orifice tube is important because it has a mesh screen which filters out any trash in your system. If its clogged, it will restrict flow and result in high pressures that are bound to damage something.<P>ANd an orifice tube is cheap...usually about $2.<P>And I believe Ogre covers all this in the Cave.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mshill||06-13-2001||04:23 PM||||I used a kit from Autozone and it worked great for about three months.  I must have a leak in the system somewhere cause now I don't think the compressor even turns on.<P>BTW, how many and where are all of the o-rings that need to be replaced when doing the R-134 conversion?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Carrolles||06-13-2001||04:54 PM||||When I did my daughter's 1988 Camry it had a slight leak that would require adding R-134 about once a month.  I put a can of stop leak dye I got from K-Mart and found the Shraeder valve was leaking.  The goop cured the problem.<P>These are the o-rings I can think of on my 85SE v-6:<P>2 at the compressor head<P>2 where the hose from the compressor meets the steel lines at the left rear fender<P>2 where the steel lines connect to the condenser under the driver's side of the radiator<P>2 one on each connection at the drier<P>1 (or maybe 2) where the expansion valve connects to the hose under the spare tire against the bulkhead<P>And I believe 2 where the steel lines connect to hoses under the spare tire against the bulkhead (it helps to remove the spare tire plastic floor to get to these.  Don't forget to disconnect this from the brake line that is attached to the underside first)<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000014||Songman||06-13-2001||05:20 PM||||You don't have to replace any o-rings...<P>All you do is change the fitting to the new QuikLoc style... Of course, if your o-rings are weak you will want to replace them.... It is best to vacuum down the system and never actually open it. If you have an air compressor this is not all that hard.<P>Then just put in your oil, proper amount of R134, and on my cars I always put in a can of sealer for good measure. This does not come with the kit but is about $5 per can. Now they also have a maximizer available too that is supposed to make the cooling closer to R12.<P>As I said, I have done 6 of these on some of my cars in the past year with zero problems... I highly recommend them.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000015||WMEBERT||06-14-2001||06:55 AM||||Hey V8 Dreamer,  Go visit the Advance Auto Parts in the Parkway Shopping Center on Lehigh Street.  We have it on $ale for $29.99 right now.  Save yourself the shipping charges from JC Whitney plus you'll have it today.  Tell them Bill sent you.  If it doesn't work out save your receipt and we'll give you your $$ back.  I'm the Division Manager for all the Advance Auto Parts stores in the Lehigh Valley and Reading and also an 86 GT owner.  Good Luck!   <P>Bill Ebert||||reg||1
Z||000016||V8Dreamer||06-14-2001||09:03 AM||||Thanks a lot everybody, you've been more than helpful....and Bill, I'll be over by this weekend to pick one of the kits up.<P>Thanks again,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fiero_24||06-14-2001||02:09 PM||||I just did one today too after reading the posts about 134.  It worked great!  My air is Ice cold, lets see if it holds the charge now.<P>Craig<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000018||Raydar||06-14-2001||02:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>...It is best to vacuum down the system and never actually open it. If you have an air compressor this is not all that hard.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Dale,<BR>Pardon my ignorance. How do you use an air compressor to vacuum down the system? What else do you need? This could save me a lot of trouble.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||06-14-2001||11:37 PM||||Aditional information on alternative refrigerants can be found on the EPA web site. It's a very good read for anyone planning a conversion. It tells you exactly what types are legal and all the fitting/labeling info.<P><A HREF="http://www.epa.gov/ozone/title6/609/choice.html" TARGET=_blank>The USEPA, CFC's and Cars.</A>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Carrolles||06-15-2001||12:46 PM||||Radar,<P>Harbour Freight (and others I'm sure) sell an eductor that is powered by compressed air that will pull down to about 28.5 inches vacuum.  I got one on sale at the new store in Birmingham for $9.00.<P>I've been told that they work pretty good but that they use a lot of air to work well, around 5cfm at 90psi and that it is pretty slow going.  You'll need a hose to connect the eductor to the car.  I'm getting the R134 hose/gauge set from Harbour Freight on sale for $59.00.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Tom88gt||06-15-2001||11:27 PM||||I have and use the Harnor Freight vacum pump.  I run it off my 5 HP 20 Gal compressor for an hour.  My compressor pretty much runs the entire time but is able to maintain good tank pressure.  So I would not recommend anything smaller. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||V8Dreamer||06-16-2001||11:26 PM||||Bill,<P>Just wanted to let you know that I picked the kit up tonight.  Rich was the name of the guy who helped me find it....I'll hopefully be doing some charging tomorrow, and I'll let you know how it goes.<P>Thanks,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000023||theogre||06-17-2001||12:35 AM||||I've covered a chunk of this in my cave. That article is presently being worked on...<P>You can often rent real vacuum pumps for not a whole lot of cash. Unless you get a screwed up one, they work better than the air driven jobs. The closer you hold to absolute vac the better.<P>One thing I used to do, pull a hard vac then close the system, shut off the pump, and let it warm back up. When you pull the first vac the system can drop allot of heat, especially if there was even a little refrigerant left. That makes it harder to remove the water and whatnot. Pausing to let it warm back up means you get more water/etc in less time. The more water out the better.<P>Pulling the vac in 2+ seperate passes like that also tells you if there are leaks. The system will loose vac quick if there's any leak you need to worry about.<P><BR>Always replace the orifice anytime the system is down. It's not worth the potential BS to save $10. If the ACC/Dryer is more than 7 year old replace it as well. Anytime a system is changed to 134 these should be replaced. The OE ACC/dryer Is Not compatible with R134. The old desicant will not work and could actually give up water to the R134. Water in the system has a nasty habit of freezing in the orifice causing ecessive head pressure in the high side. This can fry the compressor or blow out high side parts.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Songman||06-17-2001||02:35 AM||||My buddy Keith told me today about a new orifice tube that is designed for 134 systems. I have mentioned that despite what the a/c folks tell you, R134 does not cool as well as R12. Keith says this newly designed orifice tube is guaranteed to give you 10 degrees colder air. Unfortunately, all of my systems are now charged and I am not willing to lose the charge to install these tubes now. He says they are something like $3, although this may be his cost since he works for a Delco distributor.. it is worth asking about when you change your system over.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000025||theogre||06-17-2001||05:28 PM||||Would not suprise me if there's a recalibrated orifice tube. I haven't heard anything from GM about one.<P>There is also a unit called a Smart VOV. This one is a flow sensitive orifice tube that changes itselfe based on system flow. It's suposed to help allot with cooling at Idle on converted systems. You can read what I know about it from a link at <A HREF="http://www.aircondition.com" TARGET=_blank>www.aircondition.com</A>  I'm thinking of trying one out when I convert mine in the next few weeks. It's not horribly expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Songman||06-17-2001||05:49 PM||||Ogre,<P>I think that Smart VOV thing is it. I was looking at a website for them today. List price was something like $15. Keith can probably get them at cost or something. I will definitely try it in the next one i have to do. Not gonna throw away $30 worth of R134 just to try it. Luckily, Fieros are small and not that hard to cool! hehe<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Raydar||06-17-2001||11:02 PM||||Thanks, guys.<BR>I may have to give this a try.<BR>When I do my 4-to-6 swap, I'm going to have to change the compressor and lines, too. May as well do the conversion at the same time.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
